WEBVTT
TITLE Sorting Algorithms — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A sorting algorithm takes a list and returns a list. It promises two things, and the second is the one people forget.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
First promise: every neighbouring pair is in order. Second promise: the output is a rearrangement of the input, nothing added and nothing lost.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
A third promise is asked for by name: stability. Equal keys must keep the order they arrived in.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
So every question in this topic is one of: how many comparisons, how much time, how much extra memory, is it stable, and is it in place.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Selection sort scans the unsorted tail for the smallest key and swaps it to the front. It always scans the whole tail.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Insertion sort takes the next key and slides it left until it is bigger than its left neighbour. On a sorted input it stops at once.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Bubble sort swaps neighbours. With an early exit it also becomes linear on a sorted input; without one it does the full quadratic count.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
At n equals eight: selection twenty eight comparisons whatever the input, insertion seven on sorted and twenty eight on reversed.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Any algorithm that learns about the keys only by comparing them is a walk down a binary tree. Each comparison is one fork.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
A correct algorithm must reach a different leaf for every possible input order, so it needs at least n factorial leaves.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
A binary tree with L leaves has height at least log base two of L. So the number of comparisons is at least log base two of n factorial.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
And log of n factorial grows like n log n. So every comparison sort needs about n log n comparisons, and that is the wall.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Merge sort splits the list in half, sorts each half the same way, and merges the two sorted halves.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
A merge walks two pointers and takes the smaller front key each time. Two sorted lists of total size n cost at most n minus one comparisons.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Splitting n keys takes n minus one merges in total, and the tree has log base two of n plus one levels, so the cost is n log n.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
That cost is the same for every input, which makes merge sort the safe answer. It pays for that safety with a second array.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Quicksort picks a pivot, moves every smaller key to its left and every larger key to its right, then recurses on both sides.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
With the last element as pivot and a sorted input, the pivot is always the largest. One side is empty every time, and the cost is quadratic.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
A middle-element pivot does not save you. On the organ-pipe input it costs exactly n squared over four comparisons.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
With twenty five keys and a uniformly random pivot, the chance of the worst possible slot is two in twenty five, which is zero point zero eight.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Heapsort starts by arranging the keys into a max heap: a complete binary tree where every parent is at least its children.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The largest key is then at the root. Swap it to the end, shrink the heap by one, and sift the new root down.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Build is linear, then n extractions of log n each. So heapsort is n log n in the worst case, and in every case.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Everything happens inside the array, so heapsort needs no second array. But equal keys get rearranged, so it is not stable.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
The n log n wall has a condition: the algorithm learns about keys only by comparing them. Counting sort never compares two keys.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Counting sort counts occurrences, turns the counts into running totals, then places each key from the right. That costs n plus k.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Radix sort repeats a stable digit sort, least significant digit first. Each pass must be stable or the earlier passes are destroyed.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
So the honest statement is: comparison sorts need n log n, and non-comparison sorts trade that for a range or a digit count.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Here are six records with three distinct keys. Watch what happens to the two records whose key is two.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Insertion sort and merge sort leave them in order. Selection sort and heapsort swap them, because both move a key a long way in one step.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Stability matters when you sort twice: sort by name, then by marks, and the stable second sort keeps names alphabetical inside equal marks.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
In place is a separate promise: no array proportional to n. Merge sort needs n extra cells, heapsort needs none, quicksort needs a stack of log n.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Question one: what is being counted? Comparisons, running time, extra memory, swaps, or stability are five different answers.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Question two: which input? Sorted, reverse sorted, all equal, or arbitrary. The best case of one algorithm is the worst case of another.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Question three: which implementation? Bubble sort with and without an early exit gives two different answers on a sorted input.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Question four: is it a comparison sort? If yes the n log n wall applies, and if no it does not. Then look up the row.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Seven algorithms, six columns. Best, average, worst, auxiliary space, stable, in place.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Three facts decide most questions: merge sort is the safe one, insertion sort is the adaptive one, and heapsort is the in-place guaranteed one.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
The wall is the one number to remember: log base two of n factorial, which is n log n, and it holds for every algorithm that compares.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Next: hashing, the third word in this syllabus line, where the same trade-offs appear as load factor and probe count.

